# MKDIR(1)

## NAME

mkdir - make a directory

## SYNOPSIS

| **mkdir** *dirname ...*

## DESCRIPTION

*Mkdir* creates specified directories in mode 777. Standard entries, `.`, for the directory
itself, and `..` for its parent, are made automatically.

*Mkdir* requires write permission in the parent directory.

**Note:** the mode is 777 as modified by the umask(2) of the calling process, which is what
*maknode* in the kernel applies to every mknod(2). The initial mask, **CMASK** in
<sys/param.h>, is 0 on this system and nothing sets another, so a directory made here really is
777 today.

**Note:** *mkdir* is **set-user-id to root** on this system, and must be: there is no *mkdir*
system call, so the directory is built by hand out of mknod(2) and two link(2)s, and all three
are refused to anyone but the super-user. The bit is granted in *scripts/root.manifest* in
the source tree, as mode 04755.

The permission the paragraph above speaks of is therefore the program's own: *mkdir* calls
access(2) on the parent directory before it does anything else, and *access* asks about the
**real** user id. The super-user rights are borrowed for the three calls that need them and for
nothing else, and the finished directory is handed to the real user and group with chown(2).

## SEE ALSO

rm(1), mknod(2), link(2), chown(2), umask(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Mkdir* returns exit code 0 if all directories were successfully made. Otherwise it prints a
diagnostic and returns nonzero.

**Note:** a name longer than 124 characters is refused rather than copied into a fixed buffer,
which is what v7 did.
